  "strategy": "**Goal**: Produce a step function\u202fh on [0,2] (discretized) with \u222bh=1 and 0\u2264h\u22641 that minimizes  \n\\(C_5=\\max_k\\int h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\\). We must return (h_values, c5_bound, n_points) within ~600\u202fs, preferring \u22641\u202fk points.\n\n**Key observations**\n1. The objective is the maximum correlation of h with its complement over all integer shifts (in the discretization).  \n2. The constraint \u2211h = n/2 (since total length 2, dx=2/n) is linear; the box constraints are simple.\n3. The problem is a non\u2011convex quadratic program: maximize a convex piecewise\u2011linear function (the max of many linear forms in the quadratic term h\u00b7(1\u2011h shifted)). Global optimum is unknown, but good local minima can be found via tailored heuristics.\n\n**Algorithmic plan**\nWe will combine three complementary methods:\n\n1. **Deterministic convex\u2011relaxation + rounding**  \n   - Relax h to continuous variables with the same constraints and replace the max\u2011over\u2011shifts by an auxiliary variable t with constraints  \n     \\(t \\ge \\sum_i h_i(1-h_{i+k})dx\\) for each shift k.  \n   - This yields a convex problem (linear constraints, quadratic terms with a negative sign? Actually each term is bilinear h_i * (1\u2011h_{i+k}) = h_i - h_i h_{i+k}; the quadratic part \u2013h_i h_{i+k} is concave, making the constraint convex because we require t \u2265 linear - concave, which is not convex. So we cannot solve directly with CVX.  \n   - Instead we linearize the concave product using McCormick envelopes with auxiliary variables w_{i,k}=h_i h_{i+k}. This gives a mixed\u2011integer linear relaxation (MILP) after binary approximation of h. Too heavy for time.\n\n   Therefore we will **not** rely on pure convex solvers; they are ineffective here.\n\n2. **Gradient\u2011based local search on the worst shift** (similar to previous code but improved)\n   - For a given h we compute all shift correlation values C(k)=dx\u00b7\u2211_i h_i (1\u2011h_{i+k}). Identify the worst shift k\\* = argmax C(k).  \n   - The objective is locally the value C(k\\*). Its subgradient w.r.t. h is:  \n     \u2202C/\u2202h_i = dx\u00b7[(1\u2011h_{i+k\\*}) - h_{i\u2011k\\*}] (mod n).  \n   - Perform a projected gradient descent on the worst shift, with a diminishing step size \u03b1_t = \u03b10 / sqrt(t). After each step project onto the feasible polytope (box + sum constraint) using a fast water\u2011filling algorithm (clip then adjust mean).  \n   - Run this inner refinement for a fixed number of iterations (e.g., 200) starting from several seeds.\n\n3. **Population\u2011based global search (Differential Evolution + occasional swaps)**\n   - Maintain a modest population (e.g., 40 vectors).  \n   - Use **Differential Evolution (DE)** with the classic \u201crand/1/bin\u201d strategy: for each target vector x_i, pick three distinct vectors a,b,c, create donor v = a + F\u00b7(b\u2011c) (F\u22480.6). Clip v to [0,1], then enforce sum constraint by scaling and water\u2011fill. Perform binomial crossover with CR\u22480.9 to generate trial u. Evaluate C5(u); if better replace x_i.  \n   - DE is cheap (O(pop\u00b7n\u00b7shifts)) because we can compute all C(k) via FFT (O(n log n)) per evaluation. With n\u2248500 the cost is small; we can afford ~5000 evaluations within budget.\n   - Periodically (every 200 generations) inject a **local refinement**: run the gradient\u2011based worst\u2011shift descent on the current best individual, then replace it if improved.\n\n4. **Fine\u2011grained stochastic \u201cpair\u2011swap\u201d polishing** (as in original code) but with adaptive delta.\n   - After DE stops, take the best h and perform a simulated\u2011annealing style hill\u2011climb: repeatedly pick i,j and transfer a small amount \u03b4 from h_i to h_j while respecting bounds. Accept if C5 improves; otherwise accept with probability exp(-(\u0394)/T) where temperature T decays geometrically. This helps escape shallow local plateaus.\n\n**Implementation details**\n\n- **Discretization size**: Start with n=400 (dx=0.005). This gives sufficient resolution while keeping FFT cheap. After obtaining a promising pattern, we will **refine** by interpolating to n=800 and performing a short local gradient descent to ensure the bound does not degrade with finer sampling.\n- **FFT correlation**: Use `np.fft.rfft` for speed. Compute convolution of h and (1\u2011h) via element\u2011wise product of FFTs of length L = next power of two \u2265 2n. The correlation for shift k is the real part at index (k) after appropriate roll; we\u2019ll extract the first n values (positive shifts) and ignore shift 0 (which equals \u222bh(1\u2011h) \u2264 0.25).\n- **Projection onto sum constraint**: Implement a function `proj_sum_box(x, target_sum)`:\n  1. Clip x to [0,1].\n  2. Compute excess = target_sum - sum(x).  \n  3. If |excess| < tol, return.\n  4. Add `excess / n` to all entries, clip again, repeat at most 2 times (standard water\u2011filling converges quickly because after first clip max/min are hit only a few times).  \n  This is O(n) and deterministic.\n- **Gradient descent parameters**: \u03b10 = 0.1\u00b7dx, decay exponent 0.5. Stop when improvement <1e\u20117 or after 200 iterations. After each inner loop compute full C5; if it drops, keep the new h as the refined best.\n- **DE hyper\u2011parameters**:  \n  *Population size* P = min(40, 2\u00b7n/10) \u21d2 with n=400, P=40.  \n  *F* = 0.6, *CR* = 0.9.  \n  *Generations* = floor(budget / (eval_time\u00b7P)). Empirically eval_time\u22480.001\u202fs, so we can run ~4000 generations comfortably.\n- **Time management**:  \n  - Record start time.  \n  - Loop DE until elapsed < 0.75\u00b7budget.  \n  - Then run gradient refinement on the best for up to 0.15\u00b7budget.  \n  - Finally run stochastic swap polishing for remaining time (<0.1\u00b7budget).  \n  If budget is short (<30\u202fs) we scale down P and iteration counts proportionally.\n\n**Why this should improve over previous attempt**\n- DE explores the search space more globally than PSO used before; its differential mutation exploits vector differences, often finding better basins for quadratic\u2011type objectives.\n- The worst\u2011shift gradient descent is more mathematically grounded than the ad\u2011hoc backtracking line search; using a deterministic step schedule avoids wasted evaluations.\n- Combining both provides a \u201cmemetic\u201d algorithm: DE discovers promising structures, gradient refines them to the local optimum for the identified worst shift, and the final swap phase fine\u2011tunes the distribution of mass.\n- The additional refinement at higher resolution (n=800) safeguards against discretization artefacts that may artificially lower C5 at coarser grids.\n\n**Potential failure modes & mitigations**\n- *Stagnation*: If DE population converges early, we re\u2011seed a fraction (10%) with random vectors respecting constraints.\n- *Projection bias*: Repeated clipping can bias sum; the water\u2011filling routine ensures exact sum.\n- *Numerical noise in FFT*: Use `np.real_if_close` and round negligible imaginary parts.\n- *Time overflow*: All loops check elapsed time each iteration; break early if needed.\n\n**Output**\n- Return the final h array (as a 1\u2011D NumPy array of length n_points), the computed C5 bound (float), and n_points used.\n- If time ran out before any improvement, fallback to the initial_h_values (scaled to satisfy the sum) and its C5.\n\n**Summary of steps in code**\n1. Set seed, start timer. Choose n=400. Compute target_sum = n/2.\n2. Build initial population:\n   - Include `initial_h_values` interpolated to n.\n   - Fill remaining slots with random Dirichlet\u2011scaled vectors clipped to [0,1] and projected.\n3. DE main loop with FFT evaluation, keep best.\n4. Every 200 generations call `gradient_worst_shift_descent(best_h)`.\n5. After DE, run a dedicated gradient descent on best_h for up to 200 iterations.\n6. Run simulated\u2011annealing swap polishing until time expires.\n7. Optional: interpolate best_h to n=800, project, run a short gradient descent, compute C5; if it improves keep fine version, else keep coarse version.\n8. Return result.\n\nThis pipeline balances exploration, deterministic local optimization, and final stochastic polishing within the allotted budget, and is expected to lower the C5 bound below the previous 0.382149, aiming for \u22640.3807.",
